   "source": [
    "**Why profit peaks here, and it is *not* about the 6.5% cap:** at 59% approval the default rate is only\n",
    "about 5.24% \u2014 nowhere near the 6.5% ceiling. Profit still turns over because of simple loan economics:\n",
    "approving one more applicant is only worth it, on average, if their chance of default is below the\n",
    "break-even point where expected profit equals expected loss:\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "breakeven default probability = profit per good loan / (profit per good loan + loss per bad loan)\n",
    "                               = 6,400 / (6,400 + 52,000)  \u2248  10.96%\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "Applicants beyond the 59% mark have a `risk_score` (predicted default probability) above roughly this\n",
    "level \u2014 so each additional approval past that point is, on average, a money-losing decision even though\n",
    "the *overall* book is still comfortably inside the 6.5% cap. The 6.5% cap only starts to bind much later\n",
    "(around 70-71% approval, per Section 4) \u2014 by then Meridian would already be well past the point of\n",
    "maximum profit. **The recommended cutoff is the profit-maximising point, and it happens to clear the\n",
    "risk mandate with plenty of room to spare \u2014 the mandate isn't actually what's stopping Meridian from\n",
    "approving more people; the economics are.**"
   ]
  },
